WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.030
An excessive codpiece, a pair of improbable
shoes, and an iconic metal bikini.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.350
Costumes are incredibly important in creating
indelible characters.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.820
Here are a few controversial costumes that
made it to the silver screen.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.779
In September 2016, audiences got their first
look at Jumanji: Welcome to the Jungle, a

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:25.201
new adventure inspired by 1995's Robin Williams-led
adaptation of the children's book of the same name.

00:00:25.201 --> 00:00:29.979
In the photo, Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson, Kevin
Hart, and Jack Black are each wearing jungle-appropriate

00:00:29.979 --> 00:00:35.337
outfits ... alongside co-star Karen Gillan,
who's wearing what amounts to child-sized clothing.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:40.579
The photo prompted immediate criticism, prompting
Gillan to try and defuse controversy by telling

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:41.690
The Hollywood Reporter,

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.840
"The payoff is worth it, I promise!"

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.215
It was later revealed that the first footage
for the film really did explain Gillan's skimpy outfit.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.960
As THR recapped,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.359
"The plot involves four high-school students
who are forced to clean out the basement of

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:58.489
their school while in detention [...] They
find an old video game and each chooses a

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:00.059
character to play."

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:04.140
Gillan's character is a shy girl who transforms
into her adult character, and — as viewers

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.120
saw in the film's first trailer — the costume
choice is a reflection of the way video games

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.410
often portray women.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.290
Joel Schumacher's Batman &amp; Robin is usually
seen as a disaster.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.500
Setting aside the cartoony elements, the terrible
dialogue,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.110
"7 Million…Never leave the cave without
it."

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:28.040
…and poor plot choices, fans will never
forget the movie costumes—specifically the

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.770
Bat-nipples.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:31.680
Schumacher explained in a behind-the-scenes
special :

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.330
"The inspiration for them are Greek statues
that have perfect bodies.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:40.260
And, so, we're molding this perfect body in
rubber, and they're anatomically erotic.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:45.040
So, it never occurred to me not to put nipples
on the men's suits because I didn't know the

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.820
male nipple was a controversial body part."

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.950
That sounds reasonable enough—and if Batman
&amp; Robin had succeeded on many other levels,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.360
audiences might have been able to look past
the costume distractions.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.110
Unfortunately, the movie sent the entire franchise
into limbo for almost a decade, leaving fans

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.310
with years to mock the worst misstep in the
franchise's long cinematic history.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.229
An all-CGI Green Lantern?

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:12.550
Original stills and trailers for the 2011
film looked pretty awful, and although the

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.200
studio insisted it would look amazing on screen,
the animated costume has been widely held

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.960
up as one of many reasons for the film's failure.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.490
Star Ryan Reynolds later admitted to ScreenRant
that he didn't care for the CGI ensemble,

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:26.490
saying,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.540
"I mean, it's brutal doing a film where you're
wearing a motion capture suit for the whole time.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.030
[...] I didn't even get to see the Green Lantern
suit until the first trailer.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.620
I never even knew what it looked like."

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.730
The filmmakers wanted to do something new
for a superhero, and costume designer Ngila

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.430
Dickson came up with the idea that the suit
was his power manifesting outside the body—an

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.540
interesting idea, but the special effects
just didn't hold up.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.920
Dickson agreed, as reported by Entertainment
Weekly :

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.460
"We were literally doing what you would sculpt
in a studio, but we were doing it with a computer

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.580
[...] In many respects, we were following
all the same principles, but you never got

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:05.600
the tangible result that you get from the
build you do in a costume house."

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.720
Even before Pitch Perfect 3 made its way to
theaters, the production caught flak for potential

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.330
discrimination against their plus-size stars.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.740
Rebel Wilson posted a photo online of herself
alongside co-stars Brittany Snow and Chrissie

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:22.080
Fit; while the latter two were wearing sleeveless
tops, Wilson was in short sleeves.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.940
The photo prompted accusations of size discrimination,
arguably intensified since Wilson has been

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.709
a public advocate for a healthy female body
image.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.240
The controversy intensified when people pointed
out that Ester Dean, one of Wilson's co-stars,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.090
also wore a similar sleeved version of the
striped sailor top.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:43.480
Movie costume designer Salvador Perez ultimately
took to Twitter to try and put out the fire:

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.440
"I let each actor decide how their costume
fit, it was their choice.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.370
Rebel, Ester &amp; Hana Mae wanted sleeves."

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.020
In another photo posted by Kelley Jakle, each
of the stars are seen wearing a variety of

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.140
styles of the same sailor top.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.550
It appeared that the photo that ignited the
controversy in the first place may have been

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.630
taken out of context.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.300
Harley Quinn's big-screen debut in David Ayer's
Suicide Squad was one of the movie's biggest

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.410
draws, especially since Margot Robbie was
playing the famed villain.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:15.700
As with any other comic book character, a
lot of media attention focused on her movie

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.340
costume—particularly how little of it there
was.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:21.989
Robbie shared her thoughts on the tight costume
with the New York Times:

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:24.599
"As Margot, no, I don't like wearing that.

00:04:24.599 --> 00:04:27.520
[...] I'm eating burgers at lunchtime, and
then you go do a scene where you're hosed

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.932
down and soaking wet in a white t-shirt, it's
so clingy, and you're self-conscious about it."

00:04:32.932 --> 00:04:37.159
Ayers stated he didn't think denim overalls
would be appropriate for the character and

00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:40.069
insisted Robbie understood the character's
iconography.

00:04:40.069 --> 00:04:45.020
He may have a point: Harley initially wore
a jester suit in Batman: The Animated Series,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:49.169
but over the years, her wardrobe has changed
significantly as the character went from being

00:04:49.169 --> 00:04:54.919
a jester to more of a sex symbol—who's now
made her way into Hollywood, hot pants and all.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.000
"What?"

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.479
Everyone's seen photos of Marilyn Monroe standing
on a subway grate in a free-flowing white dress.

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:08.449
The shot filmed for 1955's The Seven Year
Itch was totally intentional—but the filmmakers

00:05:08.449 --> 00:05:10.870
didn't imagine it would cause such a stir.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.120
Costume designer William Travilla didn't think
it would become his most memorable piece.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.169
He took care to craft an outfit that would
convey Marilyn's sweet, innocent nature, while

00:05:19.169 --> 00:05:21.509
still letting her sexiness shine through.

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:25.909
The dress was hand pleated with metal boning
and made in an ivory crepe.

00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:30.039
It didn't take a genius to discover that filming
Monroe in public might generate some buzz

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:35.319
for The Seven Year Itch, so they shot on a
real New York street, invited 100 photographers,

00:05:35.319 --> 00:05:38.520
and let a crowd of over 2000 watch the action.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.659
Photographer George Zimbel was there and said
the crowd went "'50s wild" every time Monroe's

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:46.009
dress flew up.

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:51.513
"Don't you feel the breeze from the subway?

00:05:51.513 --> 00:05:52.513
Isn't it delicious?"

00:05:52.513 --> 00:05:55.770
The crowd loved Marilyn and she easily stole
the picture, but one man was definitely not

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.289
a fan of the stunt.

00:05:57.289 --> 00:06:02.642
Baseball star Joe DiMaggio, Monroe's husband
at the time, unexpectedly arrived at the raucous set.

00:06:02.642 --> 00:06:06.479
After watching his wife get ogled and hollered
at, he decided to leave.

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:07.860
Zimbel recounted the moment:

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.080
"They were very publicly leaving and everything
stopped for their exit.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.759
[...] There was a changed mood on the set
and everyone could feel it."

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:20.509
Apparently Monroe and DiMaggio got in a terrible
fight, and the next day on set, Monroe needed

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:22.219
makeup to cover the bruises.

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:25.729
Though the revealing dress wasn't the only
problem in the marriage, it did seem to be

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:29.409
the straw that broke the camel's back, as
Monroe filed for divorce three weeks after

00:06:29.409 --> 00:06:31.710
the infamous scene.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:35.779
Princess Leia's metal bikini struck an immediate
chord with male fans of the Star Wars series

00:06:35.779 --> 00:06:38.810
from the moment it debuted in Return of the
Jedi.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:42.809
But not everyone fell in love with the slave
Leia outfit; in fact, many viewers found it

00:06:42.809 --> 00:06:47.400
to be a prime example of objectification,
and pretty poor treatment of the series' only

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.659
notable female character at the time.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:54.059
Carrie Fisher, who played Leia and had to
put up with decades of discussion around the

00:06:54.059 --> 00:06:57.680
outfit, warned newcomer Daisy Ridley during
a discussion with Interview Magazine,:

00:06:57.680 --> 00:06:59.539
"Don't be a slave like I was.

00:06:59.539 --> 00:07:01.909
[...] You keep fighting against that slave
outfit."

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:06.240
The costume's designers insisted they never
intended the costume to be demeaning and based

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.499
the design off Frank Frazetta's artwork for
the cover of A Princess of Mars.

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:15.500
George Lucas loved the idea and since he wanted
something special for the scene, he got it.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.129
Fisher got extra attention while wearing the
getup—for more reasons than one.

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:22.599
Since it was literally made of metal, the
outfit wouldn't stay in place.

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:26.819
Though Fisher may not have loved the outfit,
she refused to be victimized by it; decades

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:30.159
later, she pointed out on Twitter that the
character's brief time in captivity ends with

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:32.599
her killing Jabba—with her own chain,

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:36.689
"That chain only 'enslaved' me [...] until
I could use the frabjous thing to KILL THAT

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:40.939
DROOLING SWOLLEN SUPER TONGUED SLUG &amp; whirl
him off into infinity."

00:07:40.939 --> 00:07:45.500
As always, Fisher had the last and best word.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:49.289
You may have blocked it from your memory,
but Ryan Reynolds played Deadpool before his

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:51.169
solo outing in Deadpool.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:55.050
Unfortunately, during his first appearance
as the character in X-Men Origins: Wolverine,

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:59.969
the filmmakers decided to make the brave choice
of doing everything they could to insult the fans.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:05.189
They took away his suit, including his mask,
and they sewed his mouth shut—leaving a

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:11.229
character who's literally referred to as the
merc with a mouth, physically unable to speak.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:15.649
Even director Gavin Hood admitted Deadpool
came out all wrong in his film, explaining

00:08:15.649 --> 00:08:20.139
it was hard to fit Deadpool's personality
into a PG-13 movie.

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:23.960
Studio ADI, who applied the creature effects
for the film, made a video explaining their

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:25.979
whole side of the Deadpool tale.

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:30.449
The artists believe the studio didn't intend
for Deadpool to be fully formed in Wolverine,

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:33.550
just a setup for what he could become in a
later film.

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:34.669
One artist suggested,

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:40.099
"If you think of this as an embryonic Deadpool,
it might make more sense."

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:44.980
From the comics to the screen, the DC superhero's
attire has been the focus of attention numerous

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:49.480
times over the years, so it's no surprise
that from the second Gal Gadot signed on to

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.339
play Wonder Woman in the DC Extended Universe,
a number of comic readers immediately chimed

00:08:54.339 --> 00:08:56.700
in about how wrong she was.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:02.240
Her performance in Batman v. Superman silenced
many who claimed she was too thin and didn't

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.759
have a hero's figure, but the controversy
didn't end there.

00:09:05.759 --> 00:09:10.350
The Amazonian's latest costume seemed fairly
appropriate, though a lot of people were unhappy

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:15.819
about the shoes; Wonder Woman has to run around
and kick butt in heels, which many argued

00:09:15.819 --> 00:09:20.050
were unnecessary and put the emphasis on sexiness
over strength.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:24.060
Though the heels might not be the most practical
thing for a superhero, the rest of the movie

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.800
costume was thoroughly thought out.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.430
Designer Michael Wilkinson spoke to "The Hollywood
Reporter" about his ideas behind her signature

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:31.430
look, saying,

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:36.420
"We wanted to create something incredibly
strong and portray her as a legitimate fighter.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.160
[...] There was a lot of love that went into
all the details, making her really look like

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.180
a powerful, legitimate warrior."

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:47.779
Though audiences generally loved the dinosaur
action and the guy carrying two margaritas

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:53.089
as he ran from prehistoric monsters, there
was one big problem with 

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:55.560
Jurassic World – Bryce Dallas Howard's heels.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.670
Howard played Claire, an uptight executive
who ends up running around in everything from

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.920
mud to wet concrete, and never for a moment
takes off her nude pumps.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.750
Even when escaping a T-rex, those heels stay
glued to her feet.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:13.130
It's not hard to see why audiences had a problem:
heels would be the worst possible shoes to

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.000
wear in that situation.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.750
The pumps must have been made of magic since
they never came close to falling off in any

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:22.130
of her off-road adventures.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.950
But Howard defended the choice to "Yahoo News",
saying,

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.720
"I'm better equipped to run when I have shoes
on my feet.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.559
[...] I think she's somebody who could sprint
a marathon in heels.

00:10:31.559 --> 00:10:35.949
[...] For me, it was actually logical for
her to be in that very illogical situation

00:10:35.949 --> 00:10:41.110
because she doesn't belong in the jungle and
yet she finds herself there and has to adapt."

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:44.750
Whether or not you like the pumps, you have
to be impressed by Howard's ability to run

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:47.500
in them without breaking an ankle.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:51.720
Judge Dredd is less than great for a number
of reasons, one being a lot of screen time

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:57.040
for Rob Schneider and another being the ridiculous
way Sylvester Stallone hollers:

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.110
"I am the law!"

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.120
But for longtime fans of the character, that
wasn't the worst of it.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:08.149
In the comics, Judge Dredd famously never
takes off his helmet, but Sylvester Stallone

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:11.149
takes it off in under 20 minutes.

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:15.399
Director Danny Cannon's original idea was
to keep Dredd dark, violent, and NC-17.

00:11:15.399 --> 00:11:19.810
He wanted the film to stick closer to the
comics and have an overall gritty tone.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:23.160
Stallone, on the other hand, wanted a flat-out
comedy.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.740
According to Cannon, Stallone wanted countless
rewrites and always pushed for more laughs.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:31.060
Plus, he had a less-than-reverent view of
his character:

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.060
"Dredd?

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:33.060
A role model?

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:34.060
You've got to be kidding!

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:35.470
This guy's a nut!"

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:39.740
In addition to making script changes, Stallone
also wanted a say in the character's fashion.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:44.839
The movie costumes were partially designed
by Emma Porteous and Gianni Versace, as Stallone

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:46.930
wanted the fashion designer's take.

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:51.430
Apparently, Versace thought the future would
run on codpieces, since they were heavily

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:54.240
featured in his original, unused designs.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.129
Alan Grant, co-writer of the Dredd comics,
said:

00:11:57.129 --> 00:12:02.089
"The story was subservient to Stallone, who,
I think, takes himself too seriously."

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:06.350
When Karl Urban was announced as the star
of the 2012 Dredd reboot, he immediately assured

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.690
fans he'd be leaving the helmet on.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:11.050
"I am the law"

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:17.160
Much darker than his usual Muppet fare, Jim
Henson's 1986 fantasy film Labyrinth is about

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:21.569
a teenager named Sarah, who gets sick of watching
her baby half-brother Toby.

00:12:21.569 --> 00:12:25.440
When her little brother gets kidnapped, Sarah
is whisked away to a magical land of goblins

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:26.790
and other creatures.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:31.329
In this other realm, Sarah matches wits with
Jareth , the Goblin King, whose romantic interests

00:12:31.329 --> 00:12:33.459
in her are deeply off-putting.

00:12:33.459 --> 00:12:39.190
His overly tight trousers and prominent codpiece
being in a children's movie is another issue altogether.

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:44.190
Jareth is complex and charismatic, a sort
of mythological rock star who's attractive

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:45.329
and frightening.

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:49.230
He's portrayed by real life rock star David
Bowie, who brought sex appeal and danger to

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:50.639
the role.

00:12:50.639 --> 00:12:55.379
Costume designer Brian Froud played up all
of that when creating the wardrobe for Jareth.

00:12:55.379 --> 00:12:58.070
He recounts this in the documentary Inside
the Labyrinth,

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:01.420
"He's [...] contemporary with a leather jacket,
it has armor on it.

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:03.230
[...] I gave him a swagger stick.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.430
It has a crystal ball, but if you look at
it, it's a microphone.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:09.389
You're supposed to be a young girl's dream
of a pop star.

00:13:09.389 --> 00:13:14.100
We got in a lot of trouble about maybe how
tight his pants were, but that was deliberate."

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.759
Because '70s and '80s rock stars wore tight
pants on stage and in music videos, almost

00:13:18.759 --> 00:13:23.069
as an aggressive display of their sexuality,
so did Jareth.

00:13:23.069 --> 00:13:27.519
During Hollywood's Golden Age, the major movie
studios all agreed to a self-policing system

00:13:27.519 --> 00:13:30.769
of censorship and adherence to standards of
decency.

00:13:30.769 --> 00:13:35.810
According to NPR, Former U.S. Postmaster General
Will Hays developed a list of 36 Don'ts and

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:40.600
Be Carefuls — nicknamed the Hays Code — that
filmmakers dutifully followed for more than

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:43.279
30 years, even though they weren't enforceable
laws.

00:13:43.279 --> 00:13:48.070
Celebrity billionaire Howard Hughes dabbled
in filmmaking and directed The Outlaw, a 1943

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:52.850
western about a vampy revenge-seeking femme
fatale named Rio McDonald.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:57.269
The movie starred Jane Russell, in her feature
film debut, and she wore a lot of revealing

00:13:57.269 --> 00:14:00.870
costumes in the film, all of which accentuated
her chest.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:05.449
Even the advertising heavily marketed Russell's
animal magnetism, using photos of her reclining

00:14:05.449 --> 00:14:10.579
on hay bales in a loose-fitting blouse without
a bra underneath to drum up the film's publicity.

00:14:10.579 --> 00:14:13.949
During production, Hughes was disappointed
that his cinematographer wasn't capturing

00:14:13.949 --> 00:14:17.139
enough footage of Russell below the neck and
above the waist.

00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:21.569
He also didn't want shots of the actor's chest
to include any trace of a bra, which was a

00:14:21.569 --> 00:14:23.819
provocative notion for the 1940s.

00:14:23.819 --> 00:14:29.689
So, Hughes used his aviation engineering background
to design a seamless push-up bra for Russell,

00:14:29.689 --> 00:14:33.060
which would be invisible underneath the actor's
wide-open blouses.

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:37.850
Hughes got the sexy shots he wanted, but once
he submitted the final cut of The Outlaw to

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:42.811
RKO Pictures, it couldn't pass the Hays Code
without the careful deletion of some of the

00:14:42.811 --> 00:14:45.800
raciest images of Russell.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:51.060
Princess Merida from Brave has been hailed
as one of Disney's least princess-y princesses.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:55.589
From her refusal to marry to her amazing archery
skills and her insistence that her curly hair

00:14:55.589 --> 00:15:00.470
run free, audiences felt Merida was a step
in the right direction—so it was all the

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:04.520
more disappointing when Disney dolled her
up later on, marking a controversial costume

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:06.699
that wasn't even onscreen.

00:15:06.699 --> 00:15:10.769
When Merida was officially announced as the
11th Disney Princess, the corporation debuted

00:15:10.769 --> 00:15:15.100
a new illustration of the Scottish lass, and
a number of fans weren't pleased.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:19.569
The 2D drawing gave Merida a slightly slimmer
figure, bigger eyes, and an all-around more

00:15:19.569 --> 00:15:21.149
glammed-up look.

00:15:21.149 --> 00:15:23.779
Brave co-director Brenda Chapman didn't mince
words:

00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:27.710
"When little girls say they like it because
it's more sparkly, that's all fine and good

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:32.180
but, subconsciously, they are soaking in the
sexy 'come hither' look and the skinny aspect

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:33.690
of the new version.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:34.699
It's horrible!

00:15:34.699 --> 00:15:38.829
Merida was created to break that mold — to
give young girls a better, stronger role model,

00:15:38.829 --> 00:15:42.970
a more attainable role model, something of
substance, not just a pretty face that waits

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:44.610
around for romance."

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:48.399
Disney responded to the controversy by saying
all the Princesses are given new looks from

00:15:48.399 --> 00:15:52.120
time to time, and that particular version
of Merida would be one of many.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:55.750
The company argued it had chosen that design
because Merida would want to "dress up" for

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:57.240
the special coronation.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:01.130
The fact that Merida hates getting dressed
up in Brave and tears apart her fancy gown

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:05.720
at the first opportunity didn't factor into
the character design, apparently.

